\title{The Two Bracketing Degrees of a Strictly Log-Concave Window,\\ and the Exact Location of the Binomial Mode}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For a positive finite sequence $a_0,\dots,a_n$ define two indices bracketing the maximum: the \emph{lower bracketing degree} $\dminus$, the first index at which the sequence stops rising strictly, and the \emph{upper bracketing degree} $\dplus$, the first index at which it begins to fall strictly. Existence of such a bracket, and unimodality of strictly log-concave sequences, are classical; the content of this paper is the \emph{explicit comparison} of the two degrees. We prove that for a strictly log-concave positive window ($a_ka_{k+2}<a_{k+1}^2$ for $k+2\le n$) one has $\dminus\le\dplus\le\dminus+1$, with $\dplus=\dminus+1$ if and only if $\dminus<n$ and $a_{\dminus}=a_{\dminus+1}$, and that the maximiser set is exactly the integer interval $[\dminus,\dplus]$. We then isolate the mechanism making both degrees computable: a \emph{threshold window}, whose rise criterion reads $a_k<a_{k+1}\iff k+1<\theta$ (weakly, $a_k\le a_{k+1}\iff k+1\le\theta$) for a single real $\theta\in(0,n+1)$. For such a window $\dminus=\lceil\theta\rceil-1$, $\dplus=\lfloor\theta\rfloor$, and $\dplus=\dminus+1\iff\theta\in\Z$. Applied to the binomial weights $\binom nk p^kq^{n-k}$ this gives a complete, explicit description of the mode in terms of the \emph{mode parameter} $\theta=(n+1)p/(p+q)$: the tie criterion is integrality of $\theta$; for integer weights it is the divisibility $(P+Q)\mid(n+1)P$; for $p=q=1$ the brackets are $\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ and $\lfloor (n+1)/2\rfloor$, with a tie exactly for odd $n$. We also prove strict log-concavity of a Pascal row, the estimate $(p+q)^n/(n+1)\le\max_k w_k\le (p+q)^n$, monotonicity and unit-staircase behaviour of the brackets, a vertex-sweep theorem, the Poisson analogue ($\theta=\lambda$), and a non-asymptotic comparison of binomial and Poisson modes under the scaling $p=\lambda/n$.
\end{abstract}

